   "subject": "Reconstruction of missing LMs using geometric reflection",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n<div dir=\"ltr\">\r\n<div style=\"font-size:12pt;color:#000000;font-family:Calibri,Helvetica,sans-serif\" dir=\"ltr\">\r\n<p>Hi All,</p>\r\n<p><br>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>I want to compare <span>in mouse crania</span> the reconstruction of missing data between the TPS method and that of geometric reflection.</p>\r\n<p><br>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>The estimate.missing() function seems to be working perfectly, however there can&#39;t be blank spaces between specimens in the .tps file. It could be interesting to mention this in the help section of the function.</p>\r\n<p><br>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>On the other hand, I am having trouble understanding the geometric reflection as explained in Claude (2008), and I was hoping maybe someone could help me out.  I&#39;m attaching the code and the .tps file for one individual with one missing 3D LM. I am lost\r\n on how to recover the coordinates for the missing LM... the code is working though, I think I just don&#39;t know how to interpret what to do after the eigenrotation.</p>\r\n<p><br>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>Any help would be appreciated, thanks!</p>\r\n<p><br>\r\n</p>\r\n<p><br>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>Best wishes,</p>\r\n<p>Javier<br>\r\n</p>\r\n<p><br>\r\n</p>\r\n<p><br>\r\n</p>\r\n<p><br>\r\n</p>\r\n</div>\r\n</div>\r\n\r\n"
    ]
   ]
  },
